<div class="doc">

<section class="panel" id="start">
  <div class="panel-head"><div class="panel-title">Quick start</div></div>
  <div class="panel-body doc">
    <p>Open <a href="/create?kind=game">Create → Game Studio</a> and choose a template. Templates are complete, editable projects — a platformer really moves, a 3D scene really renders, and a physics sandbox really simulates.</p>
    <ol>
      <li>Pick <strong>2D Project</strong> or <strong>3D Project</strong>. The badge in the toolbar shows the active mode.</li>
      <li>Press <strong>▶ Play</strong> (or <kbd>P</kbd>) to test immediately. Use <strong>Pause</strong>, <strong>Step</strong> and <strong>Stop</strong> while playtesting.</li>
      <li>Edit objects, components and scripts. Everything autosaves; the header shows the save state and version history is available from the editor bar.</li>
      <li>Press <strong>Compile</strong> (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Enter</kbd>) to validate the project, then <strong>Export</strong> to download a standalone game or publish it.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>Both modes share the project manager, assets, scripts, collaboration, input map, audio and export pipeline. They never share a runtime: switching a project from 2D to 3D changes the renderer and physics engine together.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="panel" id="tour">
  <div class="panel-head"><div class="panel-title">Editor tour</div></div>
  <div class="panel-body doc">
    <table class="table">
      <tr><th>Area</th><th>What it does</th></tr>
      <tr><td>Toolbar</td><td>Play controls, scene selector, Add object, gizmo mode (move/rotate/scale), undo/redo, duplicate, group, delete, view presets, snap/grid, compile and export. On narrow screens the toolbar wraps and the Hierarchy/Inspector become slide-over panels.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Hierarchy</td><td>Every object in the active scene, including children. Drag a row onto another to parent it. Eye toggles visibility, padlock toggles locking.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Assets</td><td>Imported models, textures and audio. Drop files here or use <strong>＋ Import</strong>. Double-click or right-click to assign an asset to the selected object.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Viewport</td><td>2D: drag objects, pan with right-drag, zoom with the wheel. 3D: drag empty space to orbit, right-drag to pan, WASD/QE to fly, wheel to zoom, <kbd>F</kbd> to focus.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Inspector</td><td>Object fields, transform, components and object actions. The <strong>Object / Project</strong> button switches to project, rendering, environment, scene and input settings.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Bottom tabs</td><td><strong>Visual Script</strong>, <strong>Code</strong>, <strong>Console</strong>, <strong>Problems</strong> and <strong>Profiler</strong>.</td></tr>
    </table>
    <p><strong>Right-click menus</strong> are available in the Hierarchy, viewport, Assets and Scripts lists. Hierarchy and viewport menus cover focus, duplicate, rename, lock, hide, add child and delete; the viewport menu also creates primitives, and the Assets/Scripts menus assign assets or manage scripts.</p>
    <div class="fi-note">Locked and hidden objects are ignored by viewport clicks, so they cannot be selected or dragged by accident. Select them from the Hierarchy to inspect or unlock them.</div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="panel" id="objects">
  <div class="panel-head"><div class="panel-title">Objects &amp; components</div></div>
  <div class="panel-body doc">
    <p>An object is a transform plus optional components. Add objects from the toolbar or the viewport right-click menu. Components are real runtime systems, not labels:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Rendering</strong> — SpriteRenderer (2D), MeshRenderer with PBR material, Light2D/Light3D, Camera2D/Camera3D, ParticleEmitter, UIElement.</li>
      <li><strong>Physics</strong> — Rigidbody2D/3D, Collider2D/3D, CharacterController3D, joints. The 3D world runs a fixed timestep; the 2D world is Matter.js.</li>
      <li><strong>World</strong> — TerrainComponent, AudioSource2D/3D, prefab references.</li>
      <li><strong>Scripts</strong> — attach a visual or JavaScript script to an object; lifecycle nodes can use the object as <em>Self</em>.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Transform fields are in world units. In 3D, position uses X/Y/Z and scale uses W/H/D. Snap and grid options are in the toolbar; gizmo mode is <kbd>W</kbd>/<kbd>E</kbd>/<kbd>R</kbd>.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="panel" id="assets">
  <div class="panel-head"><div class="panel-title">Assets &amp; models</div></div>
  <div class="panel-body doc">
    <p>Game Studio imports <strong>GLB, glTF, OBJ, FBX and STL</strong> models, images/textures (PNG, JPEG, WebP, GIF, AVIF), HDR/EXR environment maps and audio (MP3, WAV, OGG, M4A, AAC). The loaders are vendored, so imports and exports work offline.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Drop files onto Assets or choose <strong>＋ Import</strong>. Up to 20 files are uploaded at once.</li>
      <li>Select an object with a MeshRenderer, then double-click or right-click an asset and choose <strong>Assign to selection</strong>.</li>
      <li>Textures can be assigned from the MeshRenderer material fields; audio from an AudioSource component or a script.</li>
      <li>Imported models appear as mesh assets. Model animation clips are not played by the runtime yet (see <a href="#troubleshooting">limits</a>).</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Exports inline assets as data URLs. Very large models make the standalone HTML large; ZIP export keeps the runtime source and extracted assets separate.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="panel" id="graph">
  <div class="panel-head"><div class="panel-title">Visual scripting</div></div>
  <div class="panel-body doc">
    <p>Visual scripts are typed graphs compiled into runtime programs. Select a script in the bottom tab, then use <strong>＋ Node</strong> to open the node library.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Lifecycle nodes</strong> start a flow: On Game Start, On Update, On Fixed Update, collision enter/exit, key pressed, UI click, animation finished.</li>
      <li><strong>Execution pins</strong> (the diamond/arrow pins) run actions in order. One output can fan out to several inputs; an input accepts one connection at a time.</li>
      <li><strong>Data pins</strong> carry numbers, strings, booleans, entities and other typed values. Connect them to typed inputs, or type a literal value directly into the input.</li>
      <li>Connect by clicking an output pin and then an input pin (or drag between them). Click a connection line to delete it. Dropping on a node body picks its nearest compatible input.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Open the Inspector with a node selected to see its configuration and every input. The Problems tab reports type mismatches and missing entry nodes before you play.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="panel" id="values">
  <div class="panel-head"><div class="panel-title">Typed values: type or connect</div></div>
  <div class="panel-body doc">
    <p>Every data input in the Inspector has a type badge and either a <strong>value</strong> editor or a <strong>connected</strong> source. Values are stored in the node and used only while that input has no connection.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>number</strong> — numeric field; invalid input becomes <span class="code">0</span>.</li>
      <li><strong>string</strong> — text field; plain text stays text.</li>
      <li><strong>boolean</strong> — checkbox, shown as true/false.</li>
      <li><strong>any/json</strong> — text field that accepts JSON; valid JSON becomes an object/array/number/boolean, otherwise it stays a string.</li>
      <li>Connect an output to override the typed value. Press <strong>Disconnect</strong> in the Inspector to keep the connection's value and edit a literal instead.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>This is the same rule the runtime uses: a connected pin wins, an unconnected pin reads <span class="code">node.config[pinId]</span>, then its default. Use it for quick tuning (speed, damage, text) without adding a separate value node.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="panel" id="code">
  <div class="panel-head"><div class="panel-title">JavaScript &amp; Lua scripts</div></div>
  <div class="panel-body doc">
    <p>Add a <strong>Code</strong> script for logic that is easier to write directly. Code scripts run in a sandbox inside the same runtime and receive the game context; they cannot access the page or the editor. JavaScript and Lua compile to the same runtime, so either language works in Play, exported HTML and published games.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Select a script and switch to the <strong>Code</strong> tab. Choose <strong>JavaScript</strong> or <strong>Lua</strong> from the language menu. Monaco is used when available, with a plain textarea fallback.</li>
      <li>Use <strong>Visual → Lua</strong> to turn supported graph nodes into editable Lua. <strong>Lua → Visual</strong> restores the graph snapshot saved at conversion; Lua edits are not parsed back into nodes. Unsupported nodes are skipped with a visible warning and a TODO comment.</li>
      <li>Lua syntax errors are shown under the editor and in Problems; the last successfully compiled program is kept so a typo does not stop Play mode.</li>
      <li>Use <span class="code">entity</span>, <span class="code">scene</span>, <span class="code">input</span>, <span class="code">time</span>, <span class="code">log</span> and the lifecycle callbacks documented in the script template.</li>
      <li>Errors appear in Console/Problems during Compile or Play. A failing script is isolated; it does not stop the editor.</li>
      <li>Choose the trigger (start/update/etc.) in the script row. Visual and code scripts can coexist on one object.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="panel" id="play">
  <div class="panel-head"><div class="panel-title">Playtesting</div></div>
  <div class="panel-body doc">
    <p><strong>Play</strong> mounts the real runtime in an iframe over the viewport. The bar shows the mode and a live status, with Pause/Resume, Restart and Stop. <kbd>P</kbd> starts, <kbd>Esc</kbd> stops when playing.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Pause</strong> freezes the simulation; <strong>Step</strong> advances exactly one frame.</li>
      <li><strong>Console</strong> receives runtime logs and errors. <strong>Profiler</strong> shows renderer, physics/script and build information.</li>
      <li>Play mode uses the same compiled project and assets as export. A bug in Play is a bug in the exported game.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="panel" id="publish">
  <div class="panel-head"><div class="panel-title">Export &amp; publish</div></div>
  <div class="panel-body doc">
    <p>Compile first; export is blocked while the Problems tab has errors.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Standalone HTML5</strong> — one self-contained <span class="code">.html</span> with engine, runtime, project and data-URL assets. Open it from disk; no Cascade account or network is required.</li>
      <li><strong>ZIP + source</strong> — the playable build plus <span class="code">runtime.js</span>/<span class="code">runtime3d.js</span>, <span class="code">game.json</span>, extracted assets and a README. Unzip and open <span class="code">index.html</span>.</li>
      <li><strong>Publish</strong> — serves the same build at a public <span class="code">/games/:slug</span> URL. Publishing is per document; unpublished projects stay private.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Exported HTML uses a restrictive Content Security Policy and never includes editor code.</p>
  </div>
</section>

